Day 60 - Delete Node in a Linked List

Problem Statement:
Given a singly linked list and a node node that needs to be deleted. You are not given access to the head of the linked list.

The given node is guaranteed not to be the last node, and all values in the linked list are unique.

Delete the given node while maintaining the original order of all other nodes.

Code

/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(NULL) {}
 * };
 */


class Solution {
public:
    void deleteNode(ListNode* node) {
        node->val = node->next->val;


        ListNode* temp = node->next;
        node->next = node->next->next;


        delete temp;
    }
};





Time Complexity:
O(1)

Space Complexity:
O(1)

Approach:
Since we do not have access to the previous node, we cannot directly remove the given node. Instead, we copy the value of the next node into the given node. Then, we skip the next node by changing node->next to node->next->next. Finally, the original next node is deleted from memory. This effectively removes the given node's value while keeping the remaining list in the correct order.
